-- Q63: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 17, a valid contract requires mutual assent (offer and acceptance), consideration, capacity, and legality. Apex''s verbal agreement satisfies these elements — both parties agreed, consideration exists ($75,000 for services), and the subject matter is lawful.
Wrong (A): A written document is not required for all contracts. Writing is only necessary when the Statute of Frauds applies, and a services contract performable within one year does not trigger that requirement.
Wrong (C): Notarization is not an element of contract formation under common law. It may be required for specific documents like deeds but is not a general contract requirement.
Wrong (D): Attorney approval is never required for a valid contract. Parties with legal capacity may form binding agreements without legal counsel.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 63;

-- Q64: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Statute of Frauds (Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 110), certain categories of contracts must be in writing: real property transfers, contracts not performable within one year, goods of $500+ under UCC Section 2-201, suretyship promises, and marriage consideration contracts. Henderson''s land sale falls within the real property category and is unenforceable without writing; Torres''s painting contract is performable within two weeks and involves services, so it falls outside every category despite its $8,000 value.
Wrong (A): While the land sale analysis is correct, this choice is the correct answer — the question asks which statement is accurate.
Wrong (B): The Statute of Frauds does not apply based on a $5,000 threshold. The categories are based on subject matter (land, suretyship) or specific UCC thresholds ($500 for goods).
Wrong (D): The $500 threshold applies only to goods under UCC Section 2-201, not to service contracts. Additionally, oral land sales are unenforceable, not enforceable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 64;

-- Q72: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1231, net gains from the sale of business property held more than one year are treated as long-term capital gains eligible for preferential tax rates. The Section 1231 lookback rule recharacterizes gains as ordinary income to the extent of unrecaptured net 1231 losses from the prior five years, but with no prior losses, full LTCG treatment applies.
Wrong (A): Ordinary income treatment would apply only if the Section 1231 lookback rule triggered due to prior-year net 1231 losses, which is not the case here.
Wrong (C): Short-term capital gain treatment applies to assets held one year or less. Section 1231 property by definition is held more than one year.
Wrong (D): Section 1231 gains are taxable. The gain receives preferential LTCG rates but is not excluded from income.$EXPL$
